;;; expand-and-reader-time.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · error-cards
;;; Census keys touched: macro-fuel-exhausted, unexpected-eof,
;;;   unexpected-close-paren, unterminated-string (roster families)
;;; family: reader-time / expand-time   ·   *-uncounted
;;; These are REAL errors that fire BEFORE the eval boundary, so the census
;;; never sees them. This one card covers the whole pre-eval band honestly.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "error-card — reader-time + expand-time errors (uncounted)")

(error-card :key "expand-and-reader-time"
  :name "Reader-time and expand-time errors (before eval)"
  :family "reader-time"
  :census-status "reader-time-uncounted"
  :library "error-cards"
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :provenance "scheme/reference/error-cards/expand-and-reader-time.slatl"
  :status "real"

  :scheme-classification
"These are LEXICAL and MACRO errors: the program fails to READ (unbalanced parens,
end-of-input mid-form, unterminated string) or fails to EXPAND (a macro that
rewrites to itself forever). In the R7RS pipeline they occur in `read` and in
macro expansion — strictly before `eval` ever runs a single form."

  :trigger-code "(display \"abc"
  :observed-output "error: unterminated string (line 1, col 10)"
  :normalizes-to "(uncounted — fires before the eval boundary)"

  :meaning
"The error census (`stats/error-*`) is wired at the RUN / EVAL / REPL boundary:
`noteError` fires only for errors that ESCAPE a running program. Reader errors and
macro-expansion errors happen EARLIER — while turning source text into forms, and
while turning macros into core forms — so they never reach that hook. They are
real, they stop your program, they print a clear message with a line/column — but
they are INVISIBLE to the census.

This is the second blind spot (the first is silent-non-errors). It matters for
anyone reading the census as a picture of 'what went wrong': parse failures and
runaway macros will be UNDER-represented there, not because they are rare but
because they fire upstream of the counter. When you do language or module
development, you will hit these constantly and see the census stay flat. That is
correct behaviour, and this card is the note that says so."

  ;; The observed pre-eval errors — each verified, each uncounted. Note that the
  ;; roster family regexes in normalizeError were written against IDEALIZED
  ;; messages; several real messages here do NOT even match their family (e.g.
  ;; "missing )" is not "unexpected end of input"), which is a second reason they
  ;; would not land on their roster key even if they DID reach the boundary.
  :observed-catalogue (
    (:code "(define-syntax m (syntax-rules () ((m) (m)))) (m)"
     :output "error: macro expansion too deep — recursive macro?"
     :phase "expand-time"
     :roster-family "macro-fuel-exhausted"
     :roster-note "message does NOT match the family regex \\bmacro fuel exhausted\\b; also expand-time, so uncounted")
    (:code "(display (+ 1 2)"
     :output "error: missing ) (line 1, col 1)"
     :phase "reader-time"
     :roster-family "unexpected-eof"
     :roster-note "message says 'missing )', not 'unexpected end of input'; reader-time, uncounted")
    (:code "(display 1))"
     :output "error: unexpected ) (line 1, col 12)"
     :phase "reader-time"
     :roster-family "unexpected-close-paren"
     :roster-note "message says 'unexpected )', not 'unexpected close paren'; reader-time, uncounted")
    (:code "(display \"abc"
     :output "error: unterminated string (line 1, col 10)"
     :phase "reader-time"
     :roster-family "unterminated-string"
     :roster-note "message DOES match \\bunterminated string\\b, but still reader-time, so uncounted"))

  :common-causes (
    "a missing or extra closing paren — the number-one reader error in any Lisp"
    "end of file reached in the middle of a form (a dangling open paren at EOF)"
    "a string literal with no closing quote"
    "a `syntax-rules`/`define-macro` whose expansion contains itself with no base
     case — the macro analogue of an infinite recursion, caught by an expansion
     depth cap")

  :at-the-verb
"There is no verb — these fire before any verb runs. Your locator is the
LINE/COLUMN in the message (`line 1, col 10`), not a verb name. For the macro
case, the locator is the macro's own name: `macro expansion too deep` almost
always means the last macro you defined rewrites to itself."

  :how-to-detect
"You cannot `guard` a reader error inside the same source that fails to read —
the whole text is rejected before evaluation begins, so there is nothing for
`guard` to wrap. Verify this: the guard below is itself never reached, and the
reader error escapes:"
  :how-to-detect-code
"(display (guard (e (#t 'caught)) 1"
  :how-to-detect-output "error: missing ) (line 1, col 10)"
  :how-to-detect-note
"Detection is therefore a TOOLING job, not a runtime one: balance-check parens in
your editor, keep strings closed, and for macros, ensure every `syntax-rules`
pattern has a terminating case. The census will not help you here — that is why
this card exists."

  :fix-shape
"Reader: balance the parentheses, close the string, complete the form. The
line/column points at where the reader gave up (often the START of the unbalanced
form, not the end). Macro: give the recursive `syntax-rules` a base-case pattern
that stops the rewrite. These are FIX-BEFORE-RUN errors — nothing ran, so nothing
partial needs undoing."

  :lesson
"The census is a picture of RUNTIME wrongs, and runtime is not the whole story.
Errors that fire while READING or EXPANDING your code are real and common but sit
upstream of the counter, so they never appear. Two lessons: first, do not read a
flat error census as 'no problems' — parse and macro failures hide there.
Second, the roster's family regexes were written against ideal message wordings;
several real messages here do not even match their intended family. Both gaps are
honest limits of the current wiring, named here so a reader (human or model) is
not misled by an empty `stats/error-counts` after a parse failure."

  :census-verbs (
    "(stats/error-counts 20)   ;; stays flat after reader/expand failures — expected"
    "editor paren-balance + string-close checks are your real detector here")

  :related-cards ("silent-non-errors" "fuel-exhausted" "macro-fuel-exhausted"))
